<p>In the latest turn, President Trump said the United States would extend its ceasefire with Iran to allow more time for diplomacy after Pakistan asked for a pause and planned talks in Islamabad failed to come together. Trump said the truce would continue until Iran submits a “unified proposal” and talks conclude, while the US blockade around Iranian ports and the Strait of Hormuz remains in place. The extension, despite harsher rhetoric earlier in the day, lowers the immediate risk of renewed US strikes for now and keeps a narrow diplomatic opening alive. At the same time, security in the Strait of Hormuz worsened. Maritime monitors and multiple news outlets said three vessels came under fire, while Iran’s Revolutionary Guards said they seized two ships and moved them toward the Iranian coast; crews were reported safe, though at least one ship sustained bridge damage. Iran has not clearly endorsed Trump’s ceasefire extension, and Iranian officials said they would join new talks only if negotiations could deliver results and pressure on Iranian shipping eased. That leaves the diplomatic path uncertain even as both sides avoid an immediate return to full-scale fighting.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/jP10FOUChx3cgtjOVO3KwX1m13dA6f2cO7WxYD22eIQ8t8rUOr-ZspjUEuFcApMqKhxIC1X26Ztr4rL-uaDy4MU23QIwh4rYt-bJnrtBpycwb96To3dAb9UjJIV0ArqKWZbY9HtgOV_3bas_odT4i-h8cEzaubPBzyNi1gfFEzlfKZo2TTXI6mHRzLOgu2nbs63qH719eu489fS8P-kzCBdDE6udM275l4RD_b-tCkV74DVtAr-jcspXOhXWYfy_B79U0uU3pmMZQpYs6Q' alt='Ships in the Strait of Hormuz off the coast of Musandam, Oman, Monday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Pakistan mediation: Pakistan welcomed the extension after Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif and Field Marshal Asim Munir asked Washington to hold off, positioning Islamabad as the main intermediary for a second round of talks.</li><li>Oil jitters: Energy markets stayed under pressure as Brent crude climbed back toward or above $100 a barrel in some reports, while traders and executives warned that prolonged disruption in Hormuz could strain oil and gas supplies worldwide.</li><li>Troop buildup: The US is sending additional forces to the region even as it extends the truce, underscoring that Washington is pairing diplomacy with military readiness.</li><li>Iran leverage: Several analyses said the status of the Strait of Hormuz is now a central point of leverage in the standoff, with Tehran and Washington each seeking an advantage around one of the world’s key shipping chokepoints.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iran&#x27;s UN ambassador: Iran’s UN ambassador responded to President Trump’s ceasefire extension announcement, though it remained unclear whether Tehran would broadly accept it. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/22/irans-un-ambassador-responds-to-us-ceasefire-extension'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Iranian Foreign Ministry: A foreign ministry spokesman said Tehran had not yet decided whether to join negotiations in Pakistan and would take part only if it believed the talks could produce results. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/apr/22/iran-war-live-updates-trump-ceasefire-us-hormuz-blockade-israel-lebanon-middle-east'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Pakistan government: Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif thanked President Trump for extending the truce and said he hoped both sides could use a second round of talks in Islamabad to reach a broader peace deal. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1993810/at-the-request-of-cdf-munir-pm-shehbaz-trump-announces-extension-in-ceasefire-until-iran-submits-proposal'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Iranian Revolutionary Guards: The IRGC said its naval forces stopped two ships trying to cross the strait and described the operation as a maritime security action and a response to what Iranian media called a prior US capture of an Iranian commercial vessel. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/4/22/iranian-gunboat-fires-on-container-ship-off-oman-coast'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li>